+### [Ray Origin Jitter for Mesh Voxelization](15322-ray-origin-jitter-for-mesh-voxelization.md)
+Eliminates vertical slice artifacts in mesh voxelization by introducing a small, direction-aware offset to ray origins before performing ray-triangle intersection tests. It specifically addresses numerical instability in the Möller-Trumbore algorithm when rays align exactly with mesh vertices or edges, a common issue in meshes with regular grid topology such as terrain height maps. Unlike alternative approaches that bypass ray-tracing entirely (e.g., using Signed Distance Fields), this technique preserves the geometric fidelity of the original mesh while ensuring robust intersection detection.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per triangle [G]. It involves a constant number of vector operations (subtractions, cross products, dot products) and one division [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space [G]. No plane equation needs to be precomputed or stored, saving memory compared to plane-based methods [S7, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** It is widely considered the fastest general-purpose ray-triangle intersection routine for meshes without precomputed plane equations [S7, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Minimal storage, fast execution, numerically stable with epsilon checks.
-*   **Limitations:** Does not handle back-face culling internally; requires caller to check normal if needed. Precision issues can arise with extremely thin triangles or rays nearly parallel to the plane if epsilon is not chosen carefully [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per triangle. The algorithm performs a fixed number of vector operations (subtractions, cross products, dot products) and one division [S3, S10, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space. It does not require precomputed plane equations or additional data structures beyond the triangle vertices [S10, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** It is considered one of the fastest ray-triangle intersection routines for triangles without precomputed plane equations [S10, unofficial]. Optimized variants exist that delay division or handle sign checks differently, but the core logic remains the same [S3, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Minimal storage, no need to compute plane equations, robust handling of barycentric coordinates directly [S3, S10, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Does not inherently handle back-face culling (requires caller intervention) [S9, unofficial]. Numerical stability depends on the choice of epsilon and the scale of the scene [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The algorithm was presented by Tomas Möller and Ben Trumbore in the paper "Fast, Minimum Storage Ray/Triangle Intersection" (cited as 1997 in implementation notes [S1, unofficial], published in 2005 [S7, unofficial]). It was developed to provide a clean, efficient alternative to methods requiring plane equation computation, leveraging a change of basis to solve for barycentric coordinates directly [S7, unofficial]. It is a fundamental building block in ray tracing and volume rendering for determining ray-primitive intersections [S1, S4, S5, unofficial].
+The algorithm was introduced by Tomas Möller and Ben Trumbore in 1997 in the paper "Fast, Minimum Storage Ray/Triangle Intersection" [S1, S8, unofficial]. It is widely used in real-time rendering and volume rendering for mesh voxelization and ray-triangle intersection tests [S1, S7, S8, unofficial]. The method is based on Cramer's rule to solve the linear system derived from the ray-triangle intersection equation [S3, S8, unofficial].
 
